index 0000000..b05bdee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/specs/SPEC-timeparse-weeks.md
@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@
+---
+type: spec
+task_slug: timeparse-weeks
+status: approved
+created: 2026-10-15
+tags: [timeparse, spec, python, duration, parsing]
+test_framework: pytest
+interview_rounds: 0
+lane: light
+summary: "Add week unit `w` (604800s) to timeparse parse_duration and format_duration"
+---
+
+## 🎯 Intent
+`timeparse` supports `d`, `h`, `m`, `s` only, so long durations render as `14d` rather than `2w`. The ask adds `w` (7 days = 604800 s) as the largest unit in both directions.
+
+## 🌅 Outcomes
+- `format_duration` emits weeks as the largest unit.
+- `parse_duration` accepts `w` under the same rules as the other units.
+- `parse_duration(format_duration(s)) == s` still holds for all non-negative `s`.
+
+## 📋 In-Scope Scenarios
+
+### S1: Format uses weeks
+**Given** a duration of one week or more
+**When** `format_duration` is called
+**Then** `format_duration(604800) == "1w"`, `format_duration(1209600) == "2w"`, `format_duration(694861) == "1w1d1h1m1s"`
+
+### S2: Sub-week formatting unchanged
+**Given** a duration below 604800 seconds
+**When** `format_duration` is called
+**Then** `format_duration(604799) == "6d23h59m59s"` and no `w` appears
+
+### S3: Parse accepts w
+**Given** a string using `w`
+**When** `parse_duration` is called
+**Then** `"1w"` → 604800, `"2w3d"` → 1468800, `"1W"` → 604800
+
+### S4: Ordering and repetition enforced
+**Given** a string with `w` after `d`, or `w` twice
+**When** `parse_duration` is called
+**Then** `"1d1w"` and `"1w1w"` raise `ValueError`
+
+### S5: Round trip
+**Given** any non-negative integer `s`
+**When** formatted then parsed
+**Then** the result equals `s`, including at 0, 1, 59, 60, 3599, 3600, 86399, 86400, 604799, 604800, 604801, 1209600, 10000000 and every 7919th integer in 0..10000000
+
+## 🚫 Non-Goals
+- Months, years or any unit other than `w`.
+- CLI changes beyond what `parse_duration` gives for free.
+- Changing negative-input or empty-input behavior.
+
+## ⚠️ Constraints
+| Constraint | Value | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Test framework | `pytest` | existing tests in `tests/` use it |
+| Performance | no budget | pure arithmetic; the 7919-stride sweep is about 1.3k cases |
+| Security | none | no new input surface |
+| Compatibility | existing strings parse unchanged | `w` is only added |
+
+## 🔒 Irreversible Decisions
+none
+
+## ✅ Verification Criteria
+| Scenario | Verification mode | Test name / manual step |
+|---|---|---|
+| S1 | unit | `test_format_weeks` |
+| S2 | unit | `test_format_below_week_unchanged` |
+| S3 | unit | `test_parse_weeks` |
+| S4 | unit | `test_parse_week_order_invalid` |
+| S5 | unit (property) | `test_roundtrip_boundaries`, `test_roundtrip_stride` |
+
+### AC-001: Format uses weeks
+Golden values from S1.
+
+### AC-002: Sub-week formatting unchanged
+Golden value from S2.
+
+### AC-003: Parse accepts w
+Golden values from S3.
+
+### AC-004: Ordering and repetition enforced
+Golden error cases from S4.
+
+### AC-005: Round trip
+Property from S5.
+
+## ❓ Open Questions
+none
+
+## 🔍 Refinement Decisions
+- Interview skipped: the ask fixes every value, and `autonomy.level` is `auto_full`.
+- Lane `light`: 2 source files plus tests and README, one session, `irreversible_decisions: []`.
+- Oracles: the examples come from the user's ask (golden). The round trip is a property that holds regardless of implementation.
+- The spec-validator was not dispatched: the lane is `light` with an empty irreversible list.
+- Doc follow-up for execute: README line 18 lists the units and needs `w`.
